he closed alias set and cite each source.\n- **The 99999 / `bad_request` tension makes verb (5) untestable against a real `bad_request`.** `direct-id-authority-conflict` preserves 99999 unresolved, `bad-request-failure` raises `TerminalError` on any `bad_request`, and the deterministic gate runs only offline where the stub returns empty-success for 99999. So the *only* `bad_request` the suite can exercise is one manufactured by the stub, never a real absent-ID case — precisely the problem analysis \"acceptance hazard\" that a test asserting \"an error is returned\" cannot separate the correct from the masked behaviour. Achievability concern: the contract validates `TerminalError` on a stubbed `bad_request` while the same user input yields empty-success in the golden suite; the two are consistent only under the stub.\n- **`deterministic/full-test-suite` expected \"no unexpectedly passed tests\" may be unachievable if O‑8's strict `xfail` flips** (see Approach challenge 3). This is the criterion most at risk of being unmet by a faithful implementation of `conditional-distance`.\n- **`compatibility_baselines` golden obligation carries an undefined escape hatch.** \"Preserve existing … expectations **except where this work item explicitly strengthens deterministic planning**\" (`002-contract.yaml`). \"Explicitly strengthens\" is undefined, so a later golden edit could be retro-justified. The plan does not list `golden_cases.json` among changed files, so the hatch is currently unused — recommend closing it by stating that golden cases are unchanged, making the criterion falsifiable.\n- **Deterministic-in-isolation is only partially guaranteed.** `metric-and-request-checks` asserts exact request dictionaries for VC‑POC‑01/03/04 (achievable — those three schemas are supplied). But no criterion asserts *byte-identical construction across repeated runs from a fixed intent*, which is the actual determinism claim (problem analysis Q7). The model still selects the question, so suite pass^k is not a planner-determinism proof.\n- **Baseline hashes I cannot verify.** `compatibility_baselines` pins `prompts/input_catalog.snapshot.json` at `sha256:a53480…` and `rules.md`/`golden_cases.json` at specific revisions; these are harness files, not in `contract/PINNED.json`. I cannot recompute them from the provided bodies. If any pinned revision differs from the working tree, the baseline is a latent mismatch — a Work Controller check, not a critic determination.\n- **Well-formed criteria (noted for balance):** `omit-unsupplied-defaults`, `no-invented-defaults`, `safe-school-resolution`, `transactional-school-resolution`, `identity-conflict`, and the exact-request assertions for VC‑POC‑01/03/04 are deterministic, in-scope, and testable as written.\n\n## Blind findings disposition\n\n- **Resolved / addressed.** Blind Q4 (defaultable leakage) — closed by `omit-unsupplied-defaults` + `no-invented-defaults` + `out_of_scope`. Blind \"authority is the shim\" concern (Q1, partial) — `pinned-policy-authority` explicitly forbids relying on `settings.MATERIAL_INPUTS`. Blind \"collapse zero-match into Unsupported\" (Q6, partial) — `safe-school-resolution`/`failure_behaviour` require clarification for zero and multiple matches. Blind G‑4 (string-parsed conditions) — the plan's `known-condition-only` requirement derives the single condition structurally and escalates new forms.\n- **Partially resolved.** Q1 (which single artifact) — mandated to be the pinned set, but the registry/`_index`/schema disagreement for VC‑POC‑04 cardinality is not reconciled. Q5 (normalization ownership) — moved to the planner but no prompt↔planner divergence guard. Q6 (all resolution branches distinct) — absent-ID and missing-geography branches remain generic/deferred. Q7 (isolated determinism) — exact-request assertions exist but no cross-run identity check.\n- **Unresolved.** Q2 sub-question (what distinguishes a planner defect from a user-value `bad_request` in code) — the contract