{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011Cevjp2WqKHRCEB1Wvgxax","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Independent Plan Review\n\nThis review reads the current planner (`root1:harness/planner.py`), its contract accessors (`root1:harness/contract.py`), the intent shape (`root1:harness/intent.py`), settings (`root1:config/settings.py`), the existing tests (`root1:tests/unit/test_planner.py`, `root1:tests/unit/test_agent_loop.py`), the plan (`root2:planning/001-plan.md`), and the current acceptance contract (`root2:acceptance/002-contract.yaml`) against the Definition Pack and the blind analysis (`root2:challenge/001-problem-analysis.md`). I do not judge materiality, blocking status, or pass/fail; those are Work Controller decisions.\n\nOne authority question the blind analysis left open is now settled by the returned code: `harness/contract.material_inputs_for()` and `conditional_material_for()` read the pinned v0.3 `input_policy` directly, and the module docstring states \"nothing here parses prose for a 'when …' condition any more.\" So the `settings.MATERIAL_INPUTS` shim (blind Q1) and the G-4 string-splitting hazard are already gone. That narrows the review to the plan's own additions.\n\n## Approach challenges\n\n**1. \"Every planner `bad_request` is a harness defect\" is contradicted by the plan's own direct-ID passthrough.** The plan changes the `bad_request` branch to `TerminalError` (`planning/001-plan.md` → \"Change the `bad_request` branch to raise `TerminalError`\"; contract `error-boundary-refinement`). But `POC_CONTRACT_V0.3_SPEC.md` classifies \"an `acara_sml_id` absent from the pinned directory\" as `bad_request` \"because the harness violated the selector obligation,\" and the plan *deliberately* leaves caller-supplied numeric IDs unvalidated (`out_of_scope`: \"Directory-membership validation of caller-supplied numeric ACARA IDs …\"). The composition of these two choices means a user who types a wrong numeric ID has that ID faithfully passed through, the real emitter returns `bad_request`, and the planner now raises `TerminalError` — an operational crash for a user-input error, not a harness construction defect.\n- *Consequence:* a class of ordinary user errors is reclassified from a graceful outcome to an operational stop on the live emitter.\n- *Disproof:* show that no caller-supplied numeric ID can reach `fetch_evidence` without directory validation — but the plan's own `out_of_scope` forbids adding that validation, so the tension is demonstrated, not merely missing evidence.\n\n**2. The planner still repairs, so \"no repair model → `bad_request` is a defect\" is only half true.** The plan retains the headcount/non-teaching scope cleanup (`request_scope_problem`, currently in `planner.py` step 7) and adds vocabulary normalization. These *are* request repairs. If normalization is incomplete for any closed-vocabulary value, the planner will emit a request the emitter rejects, and `TerminalError` will (correctly) blame the harness — which means the plan's safety depends on normalization being *total* over the pinned vocabularies.\n- *Consequence:* incomplete alias/normalization coverage converts to crashes rather than clarifications.\n- *Disproof:* a test showing every non-canonical selector either normalizes to a catalog member or fails *before* dispatch (contract `metric-scope`, failure_behaviour \"cannot be normalized … Fail before evidence dispatch\"). Note the failure_behaviour does not say *which* typed outcome that pre-dispatch failure produces (see Contract testability §2).\n\n**3. \"Exactly one directory record per mention\" can regress answerable questions into clarifications.** Contract `safe-school-resolution` requires exactly one record before dispatch; the school-directory search is substring/fuzzy (`poc_school_directory.v0.3.0.schema.json` `request.school_search`) and returns up to `limit` records. Common name fragments (\"Grammar\", \"College\") legitimately match many records.\n- *Consequence:* a stricter-than-current rule (current code takes `resolved[0]`) turns multi-match single-school intents into clarifications — a reliability trade the contract endorses but does not quantify.\n- *Disproof:* evidence that production routes named-school answers through a path that returns exactly one record, or that golden answer cases run only through the agent loop (see §Unsupported assumptions).\n- *Distinction:* demonstrated behavioural change; whether it regresses real usage is missing evidence (no live directory sample; ADR-016 warns offline proves nothing about the live directory).\n\n## Requirement coverage gaps\n\n**1. VC-POC-09/10 \"at least one explicit filter beyond the sector default\" is unaddressed.** The registry constraints (`poc_question_registry.v0.3.0.json` VC-POC-09 `constraints`, OD-07) and golden `clarify/peer_filters` / `clarify/segment_filters` require clarification when only defaults are present. The plan derives missing inputs from `input_policy.required` (`peer_filters` present) but says nothing about the beyond-default constraint. A `peer_filters` object carrying only the sector default satisfies \"required present,\" dispatches, and (per OD-07 the emitter enforces this) returns `bad_request` → now `TerminalError`.\n- *Consequence:* an under-specified peer/segment request becomes an operational crash instead of the clarification the golden suite expects.\n- *Disproof:* a planner test asserting clarification (not dispatch) when only defaults are present for VC-POC-09/10.\n\n**2. No whitelisting of `intent.filters` against the question schema.** Contract `exact-request` builds the request from \"explicit intent filters and only the planner-owned selector.\" `intent.filters` is a free-form `dict[str, Any]` (`intent.py`). A stray or mis-named key from Stage 1 is passed through verbatim; the closed request schemas (`additionalProperties:false`) reject it → `bad_request` → `TerminalError`. The plan only validates *selectors*, not the filter dict, and only three request schemas are supplied (C2).\n- *Consequence:* Stage-1 mis-extraction of a filter key crashes the run rather than clarifying or dropping.\n- *Disproof:* a test feeding an intent with an unknown filter key and asserting a non-`TerminalError` outcome.\n\n**3. Metric labels for questions with no selector field.** Current code does `request.setdefault(\"metrics\", intent.requested_metrics)` for *all* questions. The plan restricts to the appropriate selector, silently dropping `requested_metrics` for e.g. VC-POC-05. That is safer but the contract does not state whether a mis-classified metric list should be dropped silently or surfaced.\n- *Disproof:* an observable stating the drop is intentional and tested.\n\n**4. Prompt/planner co-ownership of the \"teaching staff → teaching_fte\" alias is not reconciled.** `prompts/rules.md` (a `must_not_change` baseline) tells the model to make this interpretation; the plan moves the same alias into planner code. Both the legacy agent path and the planner path now own it independently, with no cross-check.\n- *Consequence:* the two owners can drift (blind Q5).\n- *Disproof:* a test asserting the two paths agree on the alias set, or evidence only one path is live.\n\n## Unsupported architecture assumptions\n\n**1. Runtime input-catalog load for *static* vocabularies.** The plan says \"Load/cache the input catalog through the existing client/dependencies path … and record the preflight in `deps.tool_calls`.\" But `profile_metrics`, `student_context_metrics`, and `ratios` are `static_closed` (`poc_input_catalog.v0.3.0.schema.json` `x-poc-static-vs-release-derived`) and are already available from the pinned `registry()[\"metric_lists\"]` and the pinned `input_catalog.snapshot.json`. A runtime emitter subprocess call for pinned static data introduces a data-project-environment dependency and non-determinism into the \"deterministic planner,\" contrary to ADR-016 (\"nothing offline reaches a provider\").\n- *Disproof:* the planner reads static vocabularies from `contract`/the pinned snapshot and makes no runtime catalog call for them.\n- *Distinction:* demonstrated design risk in the plan text.\n\n**2. Assumes the evidence client can serve the catalog.** The provided client surface (test mocks `_HappyClient`/`_ErrorClient` and `EvidenceClient` usage) exposes only `school_directory` and `fetch_evidence`; `get_input_catalog` is a *tool* in `harness/tools.py`, not shown on the client. The plan's \"existing client/dependencies path\" for the catalog is asserted, not evidenced. *Missing evidence* — `harness/evidence.py`/`tools.py` internals were not returned.\n\n**3. Assumes the planner is on the production path and that the golden-case baseline exercises it.** `intent.py`/`planner.py` describe a \"4-stage pipeline,\" while `test_agent_loop.py` replays all golden cases through the *legacy* `build_agent`/`ask` loop that the contract labels `must_not_change`. Whether `plan_and_execute` is wired into `run.py`/`demo.py`, and whether the `golden_cases.json` compatibility baseline (contract `compatibility_baselines`) actually runs through the planner, is not established (`run.py` not returned). If golden cases only traverse the agent loop, the planner change is protected by fewer of the cited baselines than the contract implies. *Missing evidence.*\n\n**4. \"Deterministic from the intent\" is contradicted by `deps.user_question`.** `intent.py` states \"The planner is deterministic from here: it never re-reads the question,\" yet current `planner.py` calls `request_scope_problem(deps.user_question, …)` — re-reading raw NL — and the plan preserves that scope restriction. So the planner remains partly a function of raw text, not solely of `StructuredIntent`.\n- *Disproof:* scope logic driven from `StructuredIntent` fields rather than `deps.user_question`.\n\n## Contract testability and achievability\n\n**1. Every deterministic gate is \"exit code only\" with `-k` filters that can pass vacuously.** e.g. `metric-and-request-checks` uses `-k \"metric or ratio or exact_request or normalization\"` (contract `deterministic`). Nothing asserts a minimum selected-test count or that the new behaviours are the ones exercised; a test named without those substrings, or a filter that selects only pre-existing passing tests, satisfies the gate without covering the intended behaviour. This is gameable/non-deterministic as a proof of the new behaviour, though `full-test-suite` still runs everything.\n- *Disproof:* add an expected minimum count or named-test assertions per gate.\n\n**2. \"Fail closed\"/\"Fail before evidence dispatch\" does not name the outcome type.** `identity-conflict` and the failure_behaviour \"A requested selector cannot be normalized … Fail before evidence dispatch\" leave the typed result unspecified — `TerminalError`, `ClarificationNeeded`, and `Unsupported` are all consistent with the wording. Ambiguous and not deterministically checkable as written.\n\n**3. `bad-request-failure` is universally quantified but only true under the stub.** The observable says \"An emitter `bad_request` … raises `TerminalError` … never returns Unsupported.\" The golden `answer/VC-POC-01-empty` (ID 99999, absent from the directory) returns an *empty success* under the stub but is `bad_request` under the real emitter per the spec (C1). The deterministic gate passes because it uses the stub; the literal universal claim would crash the 99999 flow live. Achievable only because the offline substrate diverges from the emitter — a known and declared limitation, but the contract text overstates the guarantee.\n\n**4. Freezing the O-8 `xfail` is an implicit, unstated requirement.** The plan touches `planner.py` only, so the strict `xfail` `test_a_radius_is_not_material_when_the_market_is_not_distance` in `test_agent_loop.py` (the *agent-loop* `_validate` gate, a different path) must remain xfailing for `full-test-suite` to exit 0. Meanwhile the planner path *does* implement conditional distance materiality (`conditional-materiality`). The contract neither states that the agent-path bug is deliberately left broken nor requires updating O-8. If an implementer also fixes `_validate`, the strict xfail becomes XPASS and the suite fails. This coupling is unsurfaced.\n- *Disproof:* an explicit contract note that O-8 remains open in the agent path and the xfail must stay strict.\n\n**5. VC-POC-03 → `ratios` routing authority is unnamed.** `contract.metric_list_for(\"VC-POC-03\")` is `null` and `intent` has no `requested_ratios`. Determining the `ratios` route requires the VC-POC-03 request schema or the input-catalog `x-poc-request-field-to-catalog-list` mapping. `pinned-policy-authority` forbids \"a new hand-maintained question policy,\" so a hard-coded `{VC-POC-03: ratios}` map technically violates it.\n- *Disproof:* the contract names the pinned artifact the routing reads from.\n\n**6. `stable-clarification` \"catalog-backed allowed values where available\" is soft.** A conformant implementation can always emit `allowed_values=[]` (as the current `_material_why` does) and claim none were \"available.\" Weak/under-constrained.\n\n## Blind findings disposition\n\n- **Q1 (REQUIRED/MATERIAL/DEFAULTABLE authority): resolved.** `contract.py` reads the pinned registry `input_policy`; the shim is removed. Plan is consistent.\n- **Q2 (bad_request → failure or Unsupported): resolved for the construction-defect case** (`TerminalError`), **but the user-supplied-bad-ID sub-case is unanswered** (Approach challenge 1).\n- **Q3 (O-8 xfail flip): partially resolved.** The plan implements conditional distance in the planner and does not touch the agent-loop gate, so the xfail persists — but the resulting two-path divergence and the implicit freeze are not surfaced (Contract testability 4).\n- **Q4 (defaulting into `request`): resolved.** `omit-unsupplied-defaults` + `no-invented-defaults`; `contract.defaultable_keys()` exists to police it.\n- **Q5 (normalization ownership + divergence test): unanswered.** Alias now lives in both `rules.md` and the planner with no cross-check (Requirement gap 4).\n- **Q6 (zero/multi/absent-ID/missing-geography): partially resolved.** Zero→clarify and multi→clarify are added; missing-geography→`unanswerable` preserved; but absent numeric ID now yields `TerminalError` (a crash), arguably worse than the current path (Approach challenge 1).\n- **Q7 (planner determinism in isolation): largely resolved.** Tests feed fixed `StructuredIntent`; exact request dicts asserted for three questions — though `deps.user_question` reliance leaves the planner not purely intent-driven (Unsupported assumption 4), and no byte-identical-across-runs assertion is required.\n- **Q8 (dependence on stub-invented fields): resolved for planner logic.** The planner reads only `records[].acara_sml_id` (a required directory-schema field) and does not read invented `result.data`; but live directory \"exactly one\" behaviour remains untested (missing evidence).\n\n## Falsifiable questions for reconciliation\n\n1. When a user supplies a numeric `acara_sml_id` that the real emitter rejects as `bad_request` (absent from directory), does the plan intend an operational `TerminalError` crash, or a graceful clarification/refusal? What code path distinguishes a planner-*constructed* malformed request from a faithfully-passed user-input error? (Approach 1; C1)\n2. Is metric/ratio/selector normalization provably total over the pinned closed vocabularies, so that a normalized request can never itself produce an emitter `bad_request` that `TerminalError` would then attribute to the harness? (Approach 2)\n3. Does the planner enforce VC-POC-09/10 \"at least one explicit filter beyond the sector default,\" or does an only-defaults request dispatch and crash? (Requirement gap 1)\n4. Are `intent.filters` keys validated/whitelisted against the (three supplied) request schemas before dispatch, or passed through verbatim into an `additionalProperties:false` request? (Requirement gap 2)\n5. Which pinned artifact is authoritative for the VC-POC-03 → `ratios` route, given `metric_list` is null and there is no `requested_ratios`? (Testability 5)\n6. Is `plan_and_execute` on the live path, and do the cited `golden_cases.json` baselines actually exercise it, or only the legacy agent loop in `test_agent_loop.py`? (Unsupported assumption 3)\n7. Does metric normalization read `deps.user_question`, and if so how is the \"deterministic from intent\" claim preserved and tested? (Unsupported assumption 4)\n8. Does the planner call the live emitter `--input-catalog` at runtime for static vocabularies, or read them from the pinned registry/snapshot? (Unsupported assumption 1)\n9. For the \"cannot be normalized\" and \"identity conflict\" failures, what exact typed outcome is required (`TerminalError` vs `ClarificationNeeded` vs `Unsupported`)? (Testability 2)\n10. Is leaving the agent-loop O-8 `_validate` bug in place (strict xfail) a deliberate, stated requirement while the planner path fixes conditional materiality? (Testability 4)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":133802,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":133802},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"df162118-92e1-4d10-986d-ab999fb27fc1","uuid":"2bd47380-22e4-42c3-9d4d-312ed343f051","request_id":"req_011CevjoyVGPfb67eRqvMvkP"}
